 FULL_FILE_EXAMPLE = """
-Before final approval, build an executable requirement-to-test matrix from the issue. For every independently stated behavior, identify the implementation location and run an ordinary-case test, a boundary or malformed-input test, and a regression test for the reported failure. Each test must assert the expected behavior—not merely that execution completes—and must exercise the public entry point used by the issue. Mark each requirement as PASS, FAIL, or UNTESTED; never treat UNTESTED or an ambiguous result as approval. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. After each test run, map every failure, unexpected pass, skipped test, warning, and traceback back to a specific matrix requirement. For each result, record the observed behavior, expected behavior, likely root cause, and the next code or test change. Re-run the smallest reproducer after every revision, then re-run the full relevant suite. An unexplained failure, skipped requirement, or test whose assertion does not distinguish the buggy behavior from the fixed behavior blocks approval.
+Before final approval, build an executable requirement-to-test matrix from the issue. For every independently stated behavior, identify the implementation location and run an ordinary-case test, a boundary or malformed-input test, and a regression test for the reported failure. Each test must assert the expected behavior—not merely that execution completes—and must exercise the public entry point used by the issue. Mark each requirement as PASS, FAIL, or UNTESTED; never treat UNTESTED or an ambiguous result as approval. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. When multiple candidate patches are available, compare them against the complete requirement matrix and consumer audit rather than selecting the most plausible explanation. Prefer a candidate only when it has direct passing evidence for every requirement, no unexplained regressions, and a clear causal explanation for the reported failure; reject candidates that pass only generic tests, rely on unverified assumptions, or leave any requirement unresolved. After each test run, map every failure, unexpected pass, skipped test, warning, and traceback back to a specific matrix requirement. For each result, record the observed behavior, expected behavior, likely root cause, and the next code or test change. Re-run the smallest reproducer after every revision, then re-run the full relevant suite. An unexplained failure, skipped requirement, or test whose assertion does not distinguish the buggy behavior from the fixed behavior blocks approval.
 
 Before final approval, enumerate every caller, subclass, override, serializer, parser, and public consumer of each modified symbol using repository search, including indirect callers discovered through imports and dispatch tables. For each consumer, trace one representative input through the modified behavior and verify that the patch preserves its expected return type, exceptions, ordering, mutation, defaults, and backward compatibility. If any consumer has not been checked, do not approve the patch. For each consumer, record its expected inputs, outputs, exceptions, ordering, mutation, and compatibility invariant, then verify the patch against that record. Actively construct adversarial cases for empty values, null-like values, duplicate values, boundary sizes, invalid types, repeated calls, inheritance or override behavior, and serialization or deserialization. Explain why the implementation handles each applicable case and add a focused regression test for every case that could distinguish the patch from the pre-patch behavior. If any consumer cannot be located or its behavior is unknown, do not approve the patch: search broader call paths, add a focused regression test, or revise the implementation. Treat changed error types, defaults, ordering, mutation, and serialization as behavior changes requiring explicit justification.
 
-- 
